---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Justuno shutting down?",
    "answer": "The Shopify listing is currently unavailable; Justuno continues to market its products. Confirm the supported installation for your store directly. Listing availability alone does not establish a company shutdown."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a new capture tool replace the sender?",
    "answer": "Only if it demonstrably supplies the required sending job. A capture replacement must preserve usable preferences, identity and separate email/SMS permission; an existing sender may remain the right destination."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can imported quiz answers start another welcome?",
    "answer": "They should not automatically do so. Define whether an import updates preferences or starts a journey, preserve the prior welcome state, and verify that replay and delayed sync cannot duplicate a queued message."
  }
];
const path = "/alternatives/justuno-alternative";
const title = "Replacing Justuno: preserve capture data and the first permitted message";
const desc = "Choose whether to replace onsite capture or repair its handoff, then reconcile quiz fields, identity, permission and queued welcomes.";
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <p class="badge">Replacement planning · November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Keep, repair or replace the capture layer</h2>
    <p>Justuno is a capture and visitor-data decision before it is a campaign-sending decision. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.justuno.com/">current site</a> promotes popups and Spotlight identification. Retaining maintained targeting and useful preferences can avoid a costly rebuild. A simpler capture candidate can reduce design and operating work when those rules no longer earn their upkeep. A sending failure after successful capture calls for a connector or sender repair first. The <a class="link" href="/apps/justuno">Justuno app review</a> covers initial fit; this page focuses on preserving existing answers, identities and waiting messages during replacement.</p>
    <h2>Resolve installation and connector availability separately</h2>
    <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/justuno-social-email-leads">Shopify listing</a> currently says the app is unavailable and directs support questions to Justuno. That does not establish that the company has stopped operating. Older installation instructions do not prove that a new store can install today. Confirm a supported route, scripts and account access with Justuno before removing a working installation. For the replacement, document theme behavior, mobile capture, cart/conversion tracking and the exact receiving connector; a logo in an integration directory is insufficient.</p>
    <h2>Inventory the promise, not just the popup design</h2>
    <p>Export targeting conditions, form versions, disclosure copy, incentive rules, question IDs, allowed answers and destination mappings. A gifting quiz, size waitlist or collector-access form has a different promise from a generic newsletter signup. Preserve what a returning visitor already answered and whether they completed the second step. Justuno’s <a class="link" href="https://hub.justuno.com/article/487-how-to-set-up-a-shopping-preference-quiz">quiz instructions</a> describe property elements and a sync workflow. They support structured preference collection, not an observed conversion gain for your store.</p>
    <h2>Write a typed field and update contract</h2>
    <p>For each field record the source ID, destination key, type, allowed values, version and owner. Map a multi-select answer to the destination’s actual representation rather than silently flattening it into a different segment. Distinguish missing, explicit null, empty string and a deliberate cleared preference. Decide whether missing preserves the previous value and whether null clears it. Reject invalid values to a visible review queue. Define precedence between a newer shopper answer, an older imported answer and a staff correction using timestamps and versions, not arrival order. Keep the raw source alongside the normalized value where supported. These are migration requirements to verify, not documented universal Justuno behaviors.</p>
    <h2>Anonymous and known identity require different handling</h2>
    <p>Propose a fixture in which an anonymous browser answers “gift,” later supplies an email, then returns with a known customer account and a different preference. Trace which profile owns each answer. Repeat on a shared device and with two addresses; do not merge people because a browser identifier or phone happens to match. A Spotlight identity match does not create email or SMS marketing permission. Keep identity resolution, preference updates and subscription state independently auditable; quarantine ambiguous merges rather than enrolling both profiles.</p>
    <h2>Separate email and SMS permission at handoff</h2>
    <p>A completed quiz or accepted email offer does not imply SMS consent. Carry each channel’s source, disclosure version, timestamp and current suppression state; keep unknown states ineligible until resolved. Recheck opt-out and purchase state at dispatch, after the handoff too. The <a class="link" href="https://hub.justuno.com/article/523-syncing-attentive-to-justuno">Attentive connector instructions</a> describe source activation and separate consent/properties. They also describe limitations around extra attributes in welcome flows. Verify those requirements in your destination account; do not apply them automatically to every connector or treat a sync success as proof of a permitted send.</p>
    <h2>Proposed retry and queued-welcome acceptance</h2>
    <p>Give a controlled submission a stable event ID, form version and original timestamp. Interrupt the destination connection, resubmit once, then restore it. Inspect actual retry behavior, duplicate detection and failure visibility; no automatic exactly-once guarantee is assumed. Let the subscriber opt out while delivery is delayed. The receiving queue should honor the newer suppression and avoid issuing a second incentive or welcome. Inventory existing waiting welcomes separately from imported preferences: mark each as canceled, retained in the old sender or transferred with an explicit owner. Never resume both queues for the same person and job.</p>
    <h2>Preserve incentive and merchant economics</h2>
    <p>For a proposed limited ceramics release, preserve the waitlist order and size/preference filters while keeping stock and offer expiry authoritative. A collector can receive access without a coupon; an incentive should not silently become a permanent VIP entitlement. For a proposed gifting store, use the answer to choose relevant education and cancel an obsolete cart reminder after purchase. These are hypothetical merchant exercises, not results. Compare incremental contribution with a suitable holdout, including capture subscription, destination fees, support, migration labor and redeemed offers. Illustrative arithmetic: a 100 sale less a 10 discount and 45 merchandise cost leaves 45 before messaging, refunds and other costs. Do not subtract the same discount again from net sales.</p>
    <h2>Choose a candidate for the demonstrated gap</h2>
    <p>Inspect <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy</a> when simpler capture could reduce maintenance; prove the targeting and fields you need. If capture is sound, investigate the sending job with <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> for SMS requirements, or <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a>, <a class="link" href="/apps/yotpo-email-sms">Yotpo</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/drip">Drip</a> for their actual account/channel scope. <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> belongs in email workflow research if that is the missing job; it is not a presumed quiz or SMS replacement. Candidate benefits are simpler ownership or a demonstrated capability gap closed, not a universal rank or forced stack.</p>
    <h2>Release by evidence and keep a reversible boundary</h2>
    <p>Assign capture, identity mapping, permission, sender queue and support owners. Compare the original and replacement on completed and abandoned mobile forms, returning shoppers, null/unknown values, out-of-order updates, expired incentives, purchase exits and disconnected delivery. Keep expected/actual logs and counts by eligible state, not only total captures. Use mutually exclusive traffic or cohorts so duplicate forms do not issue two offers. Release only after those cases pass for the supported connector; choose duration from traffic and journey delays. Keep scripts, mappings and an export for rollback. Pause the new capture and dispatch first, reconcile the last acknowledged event and waiting welcomes, then restore one authoritative path without replaying old offers.</p>
    <h2>Related operating checks</h2>
    <p>Use <a class="link" href="/alternatives">the alternatives hub</a> for replacement scope and <a class="link" href="/compare/privy-vs-justuno">Privy versus Justuno</a> for a narrower comparison. Preserve your <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission evidence</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">compliance requirements</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">destination timing</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/deliverability">delivery checks</a>. Apply <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/segment-by-value">value segmentation</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">cost analysis</a> to your actual cohort. The <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a>, <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">SMS comparison</a> and <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">cross-channel comparison</a> serve separate research jobs. The acceptance cases above are proposed, not performed vendor tests.</p>

  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
